      <h2 class="section__title">Polo Shirt Manufacturing FAQ</h2>
      
      <dl class="faq-list">
        <dt class="faq-question">What is the minimum order quantity for custom polo shirts?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          SDF Clothing offers a minimum order quantity of 300 pieces for custom polo shirts. This low MOQ allows startup brands and small businesses to access premium manufacturing without large inventory commitments. For basic stock colors with custom labeling, we can produce 300 pieces. Custom color development requires 500 pieces minimum. Full bespoke fabric production starts at 1,000 pieces.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">How long does it take to manufacture polo shirts in Bangladesh?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Standard polo shirt production takes 30-45 days from order confirmation to shipment. Custom color development adds 7-10 days for lab dip approval. Bespoke fabric production extends lead time to 60-75 days. Shipping to the US West Coast takes 22-28 days, to the UK 22-28 days, and to Australia 25-35 days. Total timeline from initial contact to delivery typically ranges 60-90 days depending on customization level.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">Can you make polo shirts in my brand colors (Pantone matching)?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Yes, we provide Pantone color matching for custom polo shirts. Send us your Pantone color codes or physical color swatches, and our lab dip process creates exact matches within 7-10 days. We match to within Delta E 2.0 accuracy, ensuring brand consistency across production runs. This service is available for orders of 500 pieces minimum. For smaller orders, we can match from our extensive stock color library.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">What polo shirt fabrics do you work with?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          We manufacture polo shirts in cotton pique (160-240 GSM), polyester pique, cotton-polyester blends, performance mesh fabrics, interlock knit, waffle knit, jersey, and premium merino wool. All fabrics are certified to OEKO-TEX Standard 100. Our organic cotton options carry GOTS certification. Performance fabrics include moisture-wicking polyester with 4-way stretch and UPF 50+ protection. Fabric selection depends on your target market and price point.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">Do you offer embroidery on polo shirts?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Yes, embroidery is our primary decoration method for polo shirts. We offer single-head embroidery for logos up to 10,000 stitches, suitable for corporate branding and team uniforms. For larger designs, we provide multi-head embroidery up to 50,000 stitches. Embroidery pricing depends on stitch count and thread count. We also offer heat transfer for small logos and screen printing for large area coverage. All embroidery work uses high-tenacity polyester thread for durability.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">What certifications does your polo shirt manufacturing have?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          SDF Clothing holds 13 international certifications including GOTS (Global Organic Textile Standard) for organic cotton, OEKO-TEX Standard 100 for chemical safety, BSCI for social compliance, WRAP for ethical manufacturing, ISO 9001 for quality management, and C-TPAT for supply chain security. These certifications ensure our polo shirts meet EU REACH, CPSIA, and other regulatory requirements. We provide certificate copies for your documentation and marketing needs.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">Can I order polo shirts with my own label and packaging?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Private label polo shirt manufacturing is one of our core services. We produce woven neck labels, heat transfer neck labels, and hang tags with your branding. Packaging options include individual polybags, retail-ready boxes, and custom tissue paper. Labeling and packaging customization is available for orders of 300 pieces minimum. For full private label programs including custom fabric development, we recommend starting at 500 pieces to optimize production efficiency.
        </dd>

        <dt class="faq-question">What is the difference between pique and jersey polo shirts?</dt>
        <dd class="faq-answer">
          Pique fabric features a textured waffle-knit pattern with raised wales, creating the classic polo shirt appearance and breathability. Pique holds its shape well and resists wrinkling, making it ideal for corporate and dress casual wear. Jersey fabric is a smooth, flat knit similar to t-shirt material. Jersey polos are lighter weight, softer against the skin, and more affordable, but less structured than pique. Pique is preferred for premium positioning, while jersey works well for casual and performance applications.
        </dd>
      </dl>
